+# Cabin injury and damaged-car ignition
+
+Cabin injury uses inward movement of cabin/roof nodes near each seat, relative to the floor. Outward dents, rigid movement of the whole structure, and front/rear crumple alone do not count as cabin intrusion. Occupant injury is evaluated after structural integration, including settling for 1.5 seconds after a hit. Damage is incremental within each crash episode, so repeated frames do not repeatedly apply the same injury. Nearby-side occupants take more intrusion than the opposite seat. Existing armour-first injury and dead-driver behaviour remain in use.
+
+`[Occupants] PlayerScale=0` in LocalDentIII.ini leaves player injury disabled; NPC injury remains enabled. Existing speed-change injury still applies independently of cabin crush.
+
+With `[Extras] Enabled=1` and `EngineReliability=1` (default), cars below 600 health, with a heavily crushed engine bay, or with severe overheating can stall and fail to restart. Stalls turn off the actual engine and prevent drive torque. Holding accelerate or reverse initiates repeated timed cranking attempts, with a cooldown after failure. NPC drivers retry automatically. Newly restarted engines get a three-second grace period. Healthy engines retain normal starting; repairs clear the failure state; wrecked and submerged cars cannot restart through this system. Engine faults also work for health damage without an existing deformation record.
+
+Validation:
+
+```
+clang++ -std=c++17 -Isrc/extras/localdent tools/test_engine_reliability.cpp -o /tmp/engine-test
+/tmp/engine-test
+clang++ -std=c++17 -Isrc/extras/localdent tools/test_cabin_intrusion.cpp -o /tmp/cabin-test
+/tmp/cabin-test
+```
+
+These isolated tests cover the state machine and intrusion measurement. They do not establish visual deformation or audible starting behaviour in gameplay.
+
+Cabin deformation uses the car model's existing body and cabin geometry, retaining its materials and textures. The crash solver bends these parts with the same node-weighted displacement as the rest of the body. No additional steel frame, cage, atomic or material is generated on impact. The former procedural cabin mesh and `CabinMetal` option have been removed; old INI entries are ignored. If a model has no inner structure behind a panel, this system does not invent one. Visual fit of the existing parts under severe deformation still requires gameplay verification.
