+# Vehicle mass and crash work
+
+LocalDent reads each car's current `m_fMass` from the engine handling data,
+in kilograms. The physical collision solver continues to own all velocities,
+impulses and vehicle motion. Deformation does not apply an additional force.
+This is a reduced visual crash-work model, not a complete soft-body vehicle
+simulation such as BeamNG. The native GTA impulse response is unchanged.
+
+The collision hooks capture relative contact-point velocity along the collision
+normal before and after the solver. Both translation and rotation contribute;
+`CPhysical::GetMass` supplies the effective mass at that contact, including
+rotational leverage. For dynamic bodies, the reduced effective mass is
+`1 / (1/effectiveMassA + 1/effectiveMassB)`. A fixed/infinite counterpart
+contributes zero inverse mass. Available normal crash work is
+`0.5 * reducedMass * max(closingSpeedBefore² - normalRelativeSpeedAfter², 0)`.
+The signed incoming relative speed must be approaching. Tangential road speed
+does not contribute to this normal crush calculation.
+
+Two deformable cars receive equal crush-work shares because model-specific
+structural compliance is unavailable. That is an explicit approximation: actual
+cars with different crush stiffness can distribute work differently. An
+immovable wall takes no modeled crush work; the car receives the available work.
+For moving props that lack modeled crush geometry, work is likewise assigned
+to the car, while the prop's inertia still affects the reduced mass.
+
+Each car's equivalent deformation speed is `sqrt(2 * allocatedWork / carMass)`,
+bounded by that car's existing solver impulse divided by its actual mass. This
+bound handles the engine's vehicle multipliers and avoids counting vehicle mass
+twice. The old deformation curve receives `carMass * equivalentSpeed` in its
+impulse slot; it divides by mass once to recover equivalent speed. Any work
+removed by the solver-impulse bound remains unassigned rather than being added
+to the other car. Allocated work never exceeds the available normal loss.
+
+A lighter and heavier car can therefore deform differently in the same crash;
+the heavier car is not assumed to always deform more. Against a rigid wall,
+doubling mass at the same speed doubles available work, while equivalent speed
+stays the same: mass-normalized crush resistance is assumed by this existing
+curve. For two equal cars approaching at equal speeds, each car receives a
+symmetrical result. A rear-end collision uses relative closing speed; a car
+matching the traffic's speed adds no crush work from road speed alone.
+
+The alternate fixed-world solver accumulates pending movement/rotation changes.
+Its hook evaluates only the current contact's increment; the engine later
+averages multiple contact responses. This is a contact-local estimate, not a
+whole-frame energy balance. An energy-adding solver response or perfectly
+elastic response generates no new plastic crush work. Existing episode peak
+budgets continue to prevent repeated contact frames from stacking full dents.
+
+GTA movement speed converts to metres per second with a factor of 50, so
+engine energy converts to joules with a factor of 2500. Captured diagnostics
+retain raw solver impulse, other dynamic mass (zero indicates fixed), closing
+speed in metres per second, available dissipated joules and the car's allocated
+joules separately from the legacy encoded deformation impulse.
+
+Run `clang++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Isrc/extras/localdent
+tools/test_collision_energy.cpp -o /tmp/test_collision_energy` and then execute
+`/tmp/test_collision_energy`. The independent one-dimensional reference solver
+checks momentum/energy for nine mass pairs and four restitution values. Further
+cases cover equal/unequal masses, exchanged bodies, walls, rotational leverage,
+glancing and rear-end contacts, elastic/energy-adding responses, impulse bounds,
+and invalid/static inputs. These mathematical checks do not establish the visual
+result of every native multi-contact collision.
+
+`python3 tools/test_collision_alt_hook.py` additionally extracts and compiles
+the actual native fixed-world solver, velocity helper and collision wrapper
+against small engine fixtures with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
+It verifies pending-output accumulation, existing vehicle/wall force multipliers,
+off-centre torque, static/infinite versus temporarily stuck bodies, the direct
+force path, rejected contacts and a disabled deformation hook.
