@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# Reflection receiver and frame consistency fixes
+
+2026-10-18. Implemented in both GTA III and Vice City Metal sources.
+
+Vehicle shaders now write their own fragment depth into the negative alpha of the motion/owner target. Reflection rays reconstruct their origin from that depth, rather than an opaque seat or pedestrian behind a transparent pane. The target is RGBA32F so depth precision near the far plane and vehicle owner IDs are retained. Motion, owner identity and MSAA coverage remain available; temporal classification accepts the new car tag and the legacy tags. Shadow/AO receivers still use opaque depth.
+
+Wet roads and static glass now receive the current frame's reflection after scene geometry and diffuse lighting. Ground materials no longer mix an unvalidated previous reflection image into the scene. This removes that source of stale foreground silhouettes and avoids sampling reflected colour back into later reflection shading. Material weights are preserved, and characters do not receive a reflection coat.
+
+No render scale, Retina setting, reflection resolution, trace distance, mesh detail, ray count, MSAA setting or temporal smoothing setting is reduced. RGBA32F metadata adds eight bytes per pixel compared with RGBA16F, including multisample storage. This is a correctness/precision cost, not a claimed free speedup. Old road reflection lookups are removed. The dynamic TLAS refit and sun integration recurrence preserve the same geometry and samples; neither establishes a gameplay FPS gain without measurement.
+
+Validation: production Metal reflection kernel distinguishes a pane from the opaque depth behind it; current-frame composite tests wet road/static glass/car/ped regions and immediate radiance changes; MSAA 1/2/4 tests preserve exact owners 7001/7002 and negative pane depths across encoder breaks, plus 8/16 capability fallback and resize; temporal reactive tests preserve stationary/moving car history and reject disocclusions. Vice City's actual vehicle shader tests preserve pane transmission, livery and paint behaviour. Both native builds pass.
+
+Gameplay acceptance remains open: inspect clean and damaged windshields, occupants, wet-road motion, transparent layering and frame cost in both games. These tests do not establish that every blue window or every source of ghosting is fixed. The legacy trails setting and temporal reconstruction can independently affect the image.
+
+
+03:21 gameplay regression: the user still observed strongly blue panes and an overlaid-looking driver after the first update. The next source correction separates glass from opaque paint's forced 60-90 percent coat and orients pane normals toward the viewer. Dielectric Fresnel starts at 0.04 (before authored specularity) and increases at grazing angles; specular highlights follow the same response. Actual Metal pixel tests compare both normal orientations and a grazing pane, preserve transmission, and verify paint remains unchanged. Gameplay acceptance remains open.
+
+
+## All car windows missing reflections: follow-up correction
+
+User report: reflections were absent from all car windows at every angle after the previous update. This remains a gameplay acceptance failure until the installed correction is checked.
+
+Confirmed pipeline defects: standard/MatFX vehicle materials did not set the glass uniform; the environment pass explicitly cleared ray metadata; and generic reflection attachments were disabled in dry weather. Glass now receives the standard traced pass, writes its own depth and vehicle owner, and emits reflection data on dry surfaces. Vehicle glass is exempt from the static-building horizontal-surface mask. Neo glass now follows the glass on/off/strength control and uses a display-strength remap of Fresnel to keep reflected scenery legible while retaining cabin transmission. This remap is artistic, not a change to physical Fresnel or trace quality; specular highlights retain the original dielectric response. Opaque paint remains unchanged.
+
+Validation: both OpenGL and both Metal builds pass. Native Metal material tests preserve cabin visibility, normal/backface agreement and paint behaviour. The full production Neo and MatFX renderer fixture uses a textured transparent pane, 4x MSAA, reduced-resolution RT preparation, ray tracing and current-frame composite. Both pipelines produce visible reflections head-on and at grazing angles; glass Off removes traced contribution. The fixture runs offscreen without starting the game. It supplies offscreen targets and enables the main-camera matrix branch for that offscreen camera; it does not verify presentation or actual gameplay temporal history. Existing render scale, Retina, RT resolution, geometry, MSAA and temporal settings are retained. Dry-weather reflection metadata adds bandwidth; no FPS improvement is claimed.
+
+Installation: all four signed binaries installed while both games were stopped; settings hashes unchanged. No game was launched.
+
