@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Sunlight scattering and MSAA
+
+Advanced graphics → Volumetric sun rays: Off / Low / Medium / High. `RenderSunShafts=0/1/2/3` in the Graphics INI section. Default is Low. Uses 8 / 16 / 32 visibility samples in a quarter-width, quarter-height volume relative to RT resolution, a maximum 140 m view segment and 250 m sun visibility ray. Existing sky/sun direction, colour and visibility drive a homogeneous single-scattering approximation (Beer transmittance and Henyey–Greenstein phase). Source: https://www.pbr-book.org/4ed/Volume_Scattering . Opaque map/vehicle geometry blocks the sun; flat roofs retain volume occlusion even when excluded from surface-shadow casting to avoid acne. Alpha-cutout foliage and glass are not opaque blockers. No multiple scattering or physically calibrated aerosol/cloud simulation.
+
+A separate premultiplied radiance/opacity texture is depth-aware reconstructed and composited after reflections and before temporal upscaling. It is not multiplied into diffuse light. This preserves MSAA, temporal AA, MetalFX, and reflection settings. Native tests cover static/dynamic blockers, flat roofs, off/night/below-horizon cases, foreground termination, sky, finite results and odd dimensions; real Metal composite readback covers colour/transmittance blending and surface/sky edges. Synthetic GPU timing is not gameplay FPS evidence. Sparse samples can lose very thin shafts; real camera motion, tunnels, foliage, weather transitions and full-scene cost need in-game review.
+
+Metal MSAA requests: Off / 2x / 4x / 8x / 16x. Unsupported levels are labelled hardware fallback; the native device selects the highest supported count at or below the request, and the FPS overlay shows that actual count. Apple M1 reports 2/4 supported and 8/16 unsupported. Tests render real coverage/velocity/reflection targets through encoder breaks and resize, and prove 8/16 requests resolve as actual 4x on M1. This cannot produce genuine native 8/16 on unsupported hardware.
+
+Whole-image softness: RenderScaleIdx 5 means 50% internal rendering; RenderScaleIdx 0 means 100%, applied after restart. It overrides the older RenderScale field. Higher MSAA smooths edges but cannot restore internal render resolution. Compare native resolution with identical temporal settings before blaming reflection resolution. Edit the INI only while the game is stopped.
+
+Run `python3 tools/run_sun_shafts_test.py`, `python3 tools/run_sun_composite_test.py` and `python3 tools/run_msaa_levels_test.py` after a Metal build. They use native GPU fixtures, not a game session. No game launch or screenshots were taken during implementation.
